      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) such that \\(g(f(x)) = 3x + 4\\) for all \\(x\\). We start by expressing \\(g(f(x))\\) in terms of \\(f(x)\\) and then compare it to the given form \\(3x + 4\\).\n\nFirst, we have:\n\\[\nf(x) = ax + b\n\\]\nand\n\\[\ng(x) = 2x - 5.\n\\]\n\nWe need to find \\(g(f(x))\\):\n\\[\ng(f(x)) = g(ax + b).\n\\]\n\nSubstitute \\(ax + b\\) into the function \\(g\\):\n\\[\ng(ax + b) = 2(ax + b) - 5.\n\\]\n\nSimplify the expression inside \\(g\\):\n\\[\n2(ax + b) - 5 = 2ax + 2b - 5.\n\\]\n\nWe are given that:\n\\[\ng(f(x)) = 3x + 4.\n\\]\n\nEquating the two expressions:\n\\[\n2ax + 2b - 5 = 3x + 4.\n\\]\n\nFor this equation to hold for all \\(x\\), the coefficients of \\(x\\) and the constant terms must be equal on both sides. This gives us two separate equations:\n\\[\n2a = 3,\n\\]\n\\[\n2b - 5 = 4.\n\\]\n\nSolve for \\(a\\) and \\(b\\):\n\\[\n2a = 3 \\implies a = \\frac{3}{2},\n\\]\n\\[\n2b - 5 = 4 \\implies 2b = 9 \\implies b = \\frac{9}{2}.\n\\]\n\nNow, we need to find \\(a + b\\):\n\\[\na + b = \\frac{3}{2} + \\frac{9}{2} = \\frac{3 + 9}{2} = \\frac{12}{2} = 6.\n\\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}.\n\\]",
