      "text": "The answer is: 10 To find the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) that satisfy the given equation \\(g(f(x)) = 3x + 4\\), we start by expressing \\(g(f(x))\\) in terms of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\). Given \\(f(x) = ax + b\\) and \\(g(x) = 2x - 5\\), we substitute \\(f(x)\\) into \\(g(x)\\):\n\n\\[\ng(f(x)) = g(ax + b) = 2(ax + b) - 5 = 2ax + 2b - 5\n\\]\n\nWe know that \\(g(f(x)) = 3x + 4\\). Therefore, we can set up the following equation by comparing coefficients of \\(x\\) and the constant term from both expressions:\n\n\\[\n2ax + 2b - 5 = 3x + 4\n\\]\n\nThis gives us two equations by comparing the coefficients of \\(x\\) and the constant terms:\n\n1. \\(2a = 3\\)\n2. \\(2b - 5 = 4\\)\n\nFirst, solve for \\(a\\) from the first equation:\n\n\\[\n2a = 3 \\implies a = \\frac{3}{2}\n\\]\n\nNext, solve for \\(b\\) from the second equation:\n\n\\[\n2b - 5 = 4 \\implies 2b = 9 \\implies b = \\frac{9}{2}\n\\]\n\nNow, we find the value of \\(a + b\\):\n\n\\[\na + b = \\frac{3}{2} + \\frac{9}{2} = \\frac{12}{2} = 6\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(a + b\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{10}\n\\]",
