      "text": "To solve the given problem, we need to find the values of \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) such that \\(g(f(x)) = 3x + 4\\) for all \\(x\\). We start by expressing \\(g(f(x))\\) in terms of \\(x\\).\n\nGiven:\n\\[ f(x) = ax + b \\]\n\\[ g(x) = 2x - 5 \\]\n\nFirst, we compute \\(g(f(x))\\):\n\\[ g(f(x)) = g(ax + b) \\]\nSince \\(g(x) = 2x - 5\\), substituting \\(x\\) with \\(ax + b\\) gives:\n\\[ g(ax + b) = 2(ax + b) - 5 \\]\n\\[ g(ax + b) = 2ax + 2b - 5 \\]\n\nWe are given that:\n\\[ g(f(x)) = 3x + 4 \\]\nEquating the expressions we have:\n\\[ 2ax + 2b - 5 = 3x + 4 \\]\n\nFor these two expressions to be equal for all \\(x\\), the coefficients of \\(x\\) and the constant terms must be equal on both sides. Therefore, we can set up the following system of equations:\n1. \\(2a = 3\\)\n2. \\(2b - 5 = 4\\)\n\nSolving the first equation for \\(a\\):\n\\[ 2a = 3 \\]\n\\[ a = \\frac{3}{2} \\]\n\nSolving the second equation for \\(b\\):\n\\[ 2b - 5 = 4 \\]\n\\[ 2b = 4 + 5 \\]\n\\[ 2b = 9 \\]\n\\[ b = \\frac{9}{2} \\]\n\nNow that we have both \\(a\\) and \\(b\\), we find \\(a + b\\):\n\\[ a + b = \\frac{3}{2} + \\frac{9}{2} \\]\n\\[ a + b = \\frac{3 + 9}{2} \\]\n\\[ a + b = \\frac{12}{2} \\]\n\\[ a + b = 6 \\]\n\nThus, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
